{
  // The `tsconfig.json` gate covers `src/`. This one covers the test suite, which
  // node erases at runtime: `node --experimental-strip-types` removes annotations
  // without checking them, so a wrong type in `tests/` is invisible until the day
  // someone runs `tsc` — and nothing ran `tsc` over `tests/`.
  //
  // `include` is restated rather than inherited because `extends` replaces arrays
  // outright. It must list `"**/*.mts"` explicitly: `"**/*.ts"` does not match
  // `.mts`, so relying on the parent list compiles zero test files and the step
  // goes green while checking nothing. `tests/tsconfig-gate.test.mts` is the
  // negative control for exactly that.
  //
  // `allowImportingTsExtensions` is required for the same reason: every test
  // imports production modules by explicit `.ts` specifier (`../src/.../sku-map.ts`),
  // which node resolves and tsc rejects without it.
  //
  // `worker.ts` stays excluded, for the reason documented in `tsconfig.json`: its
  // only import is `.open-next/worker.js`, a build artifact.
  //
  // `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is the one deliberate relaxation. The parent turns
  // it on for `src/` and `e2e/`, where an unchecked index becomes a silent `NaN`
  // in geometry or money maths; the suite inherits it here and 167 errors surface,
  // 89 of them a `string | undefined` passed as an argument in YAML/fixture
  // indexing. Landing that sweep belongs with the fixture-helper extraction
  // (buzz://issue?id=daed99a6d8d573b7e4b8df5764a87ba8a6e8aaf00956196f6419decca206595a)
  // rather than here, so the gate opts out until then:
  // buzz://issue?id=94172b4aeeb1791e513c416c85f62db681346e4d829b357b7405c8c981204c62&owner=fbe5c9f71d37efdd02a2b23c455eb4f734b7627ae8b88ffe17e1624fc2a6fec9&d=nessebar-lens
  //
  // Delete this override when that issue closes — a gate that passes because it
  // is stricter-but-broken is the failure `tests/tsconfig-gate.test.mts` exists
  // to catch, and this line is a known hole in it, not an endorsement.
  //
  // `cloudflare-env.d.ts` is excluded, and that is the whole point of this file
  // after #401. It leads with `/// <reference types="@cloudflare/workers-types" />`,
  // and that package declares `var process: any` and `var Buffer: any` as
  // globals. In a program that includes it, every `process.env` and every
  // `Buffer` is unchecked — which is why the Worker env type is described in
  // `config/env.ts` by name rather than mapped from the generated interface, so
  // nothing in the Node-facing program needs the Workers globals to be present.
  //
  // The Node program still reaches the package transitively —
  // `worker-bindings.ts` → `@opennextjs/cloudflare` → `wrangler` → `miniflare` →
  // `import '@cloudflare/workers-types'` — and lands on the package's `index.ts`,
  // a *module*, rather than its `index.d.ts`, a global script. That is why the
  // exclusion is sufficient today. It is a resolution choice we do not own, and
  // `tests/tsconfig-split.test.mts` is the gate that notices if it flips.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": false
  },
  "include": [
    "next-env.d.ts",
    "**/*.ts",
    "**/*.tsx",
    "**/*.mts",
    ".next/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next/dev/types/**/*.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "worker.ts", "cloudflare-env.d.ts"]
}